---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import { useTranslations } from '../../i18n/utils';
import Animations from '../../components/Animations.vue';
import CommentsSection from '../../components/CommentsSection.vue';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return [
    { params: { lang: 'en' } },
    { params: { lang: 'es' } },
    { params: { lang: 'pt' } },
  ];
}

const { lang } = Astro.params;
const t = useTranslations(lang);

const content = {
  en: {
    title: "Vector Search and Embeddings — From Math Theory to SQL in BigQuery",
    date: "October 2, 2026",
    subtitle: "A developer diary entry by Jill",
    back: 'Back to Home',
    intro: "Understanding vector search, text embeddings, and how BigQuery brings semantic similarity directly into standard SQL queries.",
    introDesc: "A practical guide explaining how high-dimensional vectors work, distance metrics (Cosine vs Euclidean), and SQL implementation.",
    introExtra: [
      "Vector embeddings convert unstructured text or images into numerical vectors that capture semantic relationships.",
      "BigQuery Vector Search eliminates the need to export data to external vector databases for enterprise RAG pipelines."
    ],
    section1: {
      heading: "1. What Are Vector Embeddings & Vector Search?",
      paragraphs: [
        "In traditional databases, search relies on exact keyword matching (`WHERE text LIKE '%keyword%'`). If a user searches for *'car'*, standard SQL won't match rows containing *'automobile'* unless explicitly mapped.",
        "**Embeddings** solve this by passing text through an embedding model (like `text-embedding-004`), which returns an array of floating-point numbers (e.g. 768 dimensions) representing semantic meaning.",
        `**Vector Search** measures the distance between vectors in multidimensional space. Concepts with similar meanings end up close together, enabling semantic search, recommendation systems, and **RAG pipelines** *(if you'd like to see how RAG agents are orchestrated, check out my <a href="/en/blog-vertex-ai" class="inline-blog-link">entry on Vertex AI</a>)*.`
      ]
    },
    section2: {
      heading: "2. Distance Metrics: Cosine vs Euclidean Distance",
      paragraphs: [
        "When comparing two vectors **A** and **B**, vector search relies on mathematical distance formulas:",
        "**• Cosine Distance / Similarity:** Measures the angle between two vectors. It ranges from 0 (identical direction) to 2 (opposite). Best suited for text length variations.",
        "**• Euclidean Distance (L2):** Measures straight-line distance between vector endpoints in multi-dimensional space.",
        "**• Dot Product:** Multiplies corresponding vector elements; optimal when vectors are normalized to unit length."
      ]
    },
    section3: {
      heading: "3. Executing Vector Search in BigQuery SQL",
      paragraphs: [
        "BigQuery supports generating embeddings directly via `ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDING` and querying vector indexes via `VECTOR_SEARCH` or `COSINE_DISTANCE`:",
        "Below is an example of querying text embeddings natively using SQL:"
      ],
      codeLabel: "BigQuery SQL Vector Similarity Query",
      code: `-- Generate embeddings for a search query and find top 5 matches
WITH query_embedding AS (
  SELECT text_content, ml_generate_embedding_result AS query_vector
  FROM ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDING(
    MODEL \`jill_proj.embedding_model\`,
    (SELECT 'How do I handle authentication in microservices?' AS text_content)
  )
)
SELECT 
  d.doc_id,
  d.title,
  COSINE_DISTANCE(d.embedding, q.query_vector) AS distance
FROM \`jill_proj.knowledge_base\` d, query_embedding q
WHERE d.status = 'active'
ORDER BY distance ASC
LIMIT 5;`
    },
    section4: {
      heading: "4. Practical Use Cases & Architecture Trade-offs",
      paragraphs: [
        "**Best Use Cases:** Building RAG over petabyte-scale data lakes, deduplicating customer tickets, and semantic document search.",
        `**Trade-offs:** Running vector search in BigQuery is ideal for analytical queries and batch RAG. For real-time applications requiring sub-10ms response times for live **APIs** *(see my <a href="/en/blog-apis" class="inline-blog-link">entry on APIs</a>)*, dedicated vector stores like Pinecone, Qdrant, or Pgvector remain superior.`
      ]
    }
  },
  es: {
    title: "Búsqueda Vectorial y Embeddings — De la Teoría a SQL en BigQuery",
    date: "2 de Octubre, 2026",
    subtitle: "El diario de desarrollo de Jill",
    back: 'Volver al Inicio',
    intro: "Una explicación clara sobre búsqueda vectorial, embeddings de texto y cómo BigQuery integra la similitud semántica en consultas SQL.",
    introDesc: "Guía práctica sobre vectores de alta dimensión, métricas de distancia (Coseno vs Euclidiana) e implementación en bases de datos.",
    introExtra: [
      "Los embeddings convierten texto e imágenes en vectores numéricos que capturan relaciones de significado.",
      "BigQuery Vector Search permite hacer búsquedas semánticas sobre petabytes de datos sin requerir bases de datos vectoriales externas."
    ],
    section1: {
      heading: "1. ¿Qué son los Embeddings y la Búsqueda Vectorial?",
      paragraphs: [
        "En bases de datos tradicionales, la búsqueda se basa en coincidencias exactas (`WHERE texto LIKE '%palabra%'`). Si un usuario busca *'auto'*, SQL estándar no encontrará registros con la palabra *'vehículo'* a menos que se configure explícitamente.",
        "Los **Embeddings** resuelven esto convirtiendo texto a través de un modelo de embedding (como `text-embedding-004`) en un arreglo de números flotantes (por ejemplo, 768 dimensiones) que representan su significado semántico.",
        `La **Búsqueda Vectorial** calcula la distancia entre vectores en un espacio multidimensional. Los conceptos similares quedan cercanos entre sí, lo que habilita la búsqueda por contexto y sistemas de **RAG** *(si quieres aprender cómo se orquestan agentes con RAG, revisa mi <a href="/es/blog-vertex-ai" class="inline-blog-link">entrada sobre Vertex AI</a>)*.`
      ]
    },
    section2: {
      heading: "2. Métricas de Distancia: Coseno vs Euclidiana",
      paragraphs: [
        "Para comparar dos vectores **A** y **B**, se utilizan fórmulas matemáticas de distancia:",
        "**• Distancia Coseno:** Mide el ángulo entre dos vectores. Varía entre 0 (misma dirección) y 2 (opuesta). Es la más utilizada en procesamiento de lenguaje natural.",
        "**• Distancia Euclidiana (L2):** Mide la distancia en línea recta entre los puntos en el espacio multidimensional.",
        "**• Producto Punto:** Multiplica los elementos correspondientes; es eficiente cuando los vectores están normalizados."
      ]
    },
    section3: {
      heading: "3. Ejecutando Búsquedas Vectoriales en BigQuery SQL",
      paragraphs: [
        "BigQuery permite generar embeddings directamente mediante `ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDING` y consultar vectores con `COSINE_DISTANCE`:",
        "Ejemplo de consulta de similitud vectorial en SQL:"
      ],
      codeLabel: "Consulta SQL de Similitud Vectorial",
      code: `-- Generar embedding de búsqueda y consultar las 5 coincidencias más cercanas
WITH query_embedding AS (
  SELECT text_content, ml_generate_embedding_result AS query_vector
  FROM ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDING(
    MODEL \`jill_proj.embedding_model\`,
    (SELECT '¿Cómo manejar la autenticación en microservicios?' AS text_content)
  )
)
SELECT 
  d.doc_id,
  d.title,
  COSINE_DISTANCE(d.embedding, q.query_vector) AS distance
FROM \`jill_proj.knowledge_base\` d, query_embedding q
WHERE d.status = 'active'
ORDER BY distance ASC
LIMIT 5;`
    },
    section4: {
      heading: "4. Casos de Uso y Comparativa Arquitectónica",
      paragraphs: [
        "**Casos ideales:** Implementación de RAG sobre almacenes de datos a escala empresarial, deduplicación de registros y búsqueda en bases de conocimiento.",
        `**Consideraciones:** Usar BigQuery para búsqueda vectorial es ideal en análisis y batch RAG. Para **APIs** *(revisa mi <a href="/es/blog-apis" class="inline-blog-link">diario sobre APIs como conversaciones</a>)* interactivas en tiempo real que exijan milisegundos de un solo dígito, bases vectoriales dedicadas (como Qdrant o Pgvector) siguen siendo la opción preferida.`
      ]
    }
  },
  pt: {
    title: "Busca Vetorial e Embeddings — Da Teoria ao SQL no BigQuery",
    date: "2 de Outubro, 2026",
    subtitle: "O diário de desenvolvimento de Jill",
    back: 'Voltar ao Início',
    intro: "Uma explicação técnica sobre busca vetorial, embeddings de texto e como o BigQuery integra busca semântica em consultas SQL.",
    introDesc: "Guia prático sobre vetores multidimensionais, métricas de distância e implementação em bancos de dados.",
    introExtra: [
      "Embeddings transformam texto em vetores numéricos que capturam relações de significado.",
      "O BigQuery Vector Search permite consultas semânticas sem necessidade de exportar dados para bancos vetoriais dedicados."
    ],
    section1: {
      heading: "1. O que são Embeddings e Busca Vetorial?",
      paragraphs: [
        "Em bancos tradicionais, a busca depende de correspondência exata de palavras (`WHERE text LIKE '%palavra%'`).",
        "Os **Embeddings** resolvem isso convertendo o texto por meio de um modelo numérico em vetores multidimensionais que representam o contexto da frase.",
        `A **Busca Vetorial** mede a distância entre esses vetores no espaço numérico, permitindo criar fluxos de **RAG** *(se quiser entender agentes com RAG, veja meu <a href="/pt/blog-vertex-ai" class="inline-blog-link">post sobre Vertex AI</a>)*.`
      ]
    },
    section2: {
      heading: "2. Métricas de Distância",
      paragraphs: [
        "**• Distância Cosseno:** Mede o ângulo entre dois vetores. Ideal para comparação de textos de tamanhos variados.",
        "**• Distância Euclidiana (L2):** Mede a distância em linha reta no espaço multidimensional.",
        "**• Produto Escalar:** Multiplicação direta de elementos, ideal para vetores normalizados."
      ]
    },
    section3: {
      heading: "3. Consulta de Busca Vetorial no BigQuery SQL",
      paragraphs: [
        "Exemplo de consulta SQL nativa no BigQuery utilizando funções de distância Cosseno:"
      ],
      codeLabel: "Consulta de Busca Vetorial em SQL",
      code: `WITH query_embedding AS (
  SELECT text_content, ml_generate_embedding_result AS query_vector
  FROM ML.GENERATE_EMBEDDING(
    MODEL \`jill_proj.embedding_model\`,
    (SELECT 'Como gerenciar autenticação em microsserviços?' AS text_content)
  )
)
SELECT 
  d.doc_id,
  d.title,
  COSINE_DISTANCE(d.embedding, q.query_vector) AS distance
FROM \`jill_proj.knowledge_base\` d, query_embedding q
WHERE d.status = 'active'
ORDER BY distance ASC
LIMIT 5;`
    },
    section4: {
      heading: "4. Casos de Uso e Considerações",
      paragraphs: [
        "**Casos de uso:** RAG corporativo sobre grandes volumes de dados e busca de documentos por significado.",
        `**Considerações:** Para **APIs** *(veja meu <a href="/pt/blog-apis" class="inline-blog-link">post sobre APIs</a>)* em tempo real abaixo de 10ms, bancos vetoriais dedicados continuam sendo recomendados.`
      ]
    }
  }
};

const c = content[lang] || content.es;
---

<Layout title={`${c.title} | Jill Portfolio`} lang={lang}>
  <Animations client:load />
  
  <div class="blog-post-page">
    <Nav activeSection="blog" lang={lang} />
    
    <main class="container">
      <article class="post-article">
        <a href={`/${lang}/#blog`} class="back-link">
          <span class="arrow">←</span> {c.back}
        </a>

        <header class="post-header">
          <div class="meta-badge">
            Data & Vector Search
          </div>
          <h1 class="post-title">{c.title}</h1>
          <p class="post-subtitle">{c.subtitle}</p>
          <div class="post-date">
            <span class="calendar-icon">📅</span> {c.date}
          </div>
        </header>

        <div class="post-card">
          <section class="intro-section">
            <div class="intro-bubble">
              <p class="intro-main">{c.intro}</p>
              <p class="intro-desc">{c.introDesc}</p>
            </div>
            
            <div class="extra-bullets">
              {c.introExtra.map(bullet => (
                <div class="bullet-item">
                  <span class="bullet-icon">•</span>
                  <span>{bullet}</span>
                </div>
              ))}
            </div>
          </section>

          <hr class="divider" />

          <section class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-heading">{c.section1.heading}</h2>
            {c.section1.paragraphs.map(p => (
              <p class="section-paragraph" set:html={p.replace(/\*\*(.*?)\*\*/g, '<strong>$1</strong>')} />
            ))}
          </section>

          <section class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-heading">{c.section2.heading}</h2>
            {c.section2.paragraphs.map(p => (
              <p class="section-paragraph" set:html={p.replace(/\*\*(.*?)\*\*/g, '<strong>$1</strong>')} />
            ))}
          </section>

          <section class="content-section">
            <h2 class="section-heading">{c.section3.heading}</h2>
            {c.section3.paragraphs.map(p => (
              <p class="section-paragraph" set:html={p.replace(/\*\*(.*?)\*\*/g, '<strong>$1</strong>')} />
            ))}

            <div class="code-box">
              <div class="code-header">
                <span class="code-dot red"></span>
                <span class="code-dot yellow"></span>
                <span class="code-dot green"></span>
                <span class="code-title">{c.section3.codeLabel}</span>
              </div>
              <pre class="code-block"><code>{c.section3.code}</code></pre>
            </div>
          </section>

          <section class="content-section conclusion-box">
            <h2 class="section-heading">{c.section4.heading}</h2>
            {c.section4.paragraphs.map(p => (
              <p class="section-paragraph" set:html={p.replace(/\*\*(.*?)\*\*/g, '<strong>$1</strong>')} />
            ))}
          </section>

          <section class="comments-wrapper">
            <CommentsSection client:load postSlug="blog-vector-search" currentLang={lang} />
          </section>
        </div>
      </article>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  .blog-post-page {
    min-height: 100vh;
    background: radial-gradient(circle at top center, rgba(148, 163, 184, 0.1) 0%, rgba(15, 15, 26, 0.98) 70%);
    color: #e2e8f0;
    padding-bottom: 5rem;
  }

  .container {
    max-width: 900px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 2rem 1.5rem;
  }

  .back-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    color: #38bdf8;
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
    transition: all 0.2s ease;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: #0284c7;
    transform: translateX(-4px);
  }

  .post-header {
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .meta-badge {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    padding: 0.4rem 1rem;
    background: rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.12);
    border: 1px solid rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.25);
    border-radius: 9999px;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: #38bdf8;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .post-title {
    font-size: 2.25rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    line-height: 1.25;
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, #ffffff 0%, #cbd5e1 100%);
    -webkit-background-clip: text;
    -webkit-text-fill-color: transparent;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .post-subtitle {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    color: #94a3b8;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .post-date {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.4rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: #cbd5e1;
  }

  .post-card {
    background: rgba(30, 41, 59, 0.7);
    backdrop-filter: blur(12px);
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
    border-radius: 1.5rem;
    padding: 2.5rem;
    box-shadow: 0 20px 40px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.3);
  }

  .intro-bubble {
    background: rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.08);
    border-left: 4px solid #38bdf8;
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .intro-main {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: #f8fafc;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .intro-desc {
    color: #cbd5e1;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .extra-bullets {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .bullet-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    color: #e2e8f0;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .bullet-icon {
    color: #38bdf8;
  }

  .divider {
    border: 0;
    height: 1px;
    background: linear-gradient(90deg, transparent, rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.3), transparent);
    margin: 2.5rem 0;
  }

  .content-section {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .section-heading {
    font-size: 1.4rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: #f8fafc;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .section-paragraph {
    font-size: 1rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: #cbd5e1;
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  :global(.inline-blog-link) {
    color: #38bdf8;
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 3px;
    font-weight: 500;
    transition: color 0.2s ease;
  }
  :global(.inline-blog-link:hover) {
    color: #7dd3fc;
  }

  .code-box {
    background: #0f172a;
    border: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
    border-radius: 0.75rem;
    overflow: hidden;
    margin: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .code-header {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.6rem 1rem;
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.05);
  }

  .code-dot {
    width: 10px;
    height: 10px;
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  .code-dot.red { background: #ef4444; }
  .code-dot.yellow { background: #eab308; }
  .code-dot.green { background: #22c55e; }

  .code-title {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-family: monospace;
    color: #94a3b8;
    margin-left: 0.5rem;
  }

  .code-block {
    padding: 1.25rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-family: 'Fira Code', 'Courier New', monospace;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: #38bdf8;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .conclusion-box {
    background: rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.05);
    border: 1px solid rgba(56, 189, 248, 0.2);
    border-radius: 1rem;
    padding: 1.75rem;
  }

  .comments-wrapper {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 2rem;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.1);
  }

  @media (max-width: 640px) {
    .post-title {
      font-size: 1.75rem;
    }
    .post-card {
      padding: 1.5rem;
    }
  }
</style>
